        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1447">

                <pb facs="1169" n="1053" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1053"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.00/2028.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chief of Staff of the War
                            Department to the Adjutant
                    General</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">War Department</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-09-30">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 30, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram forwarded by the War Department to Colonel Goethals
                    September 30, 1912; copy sent to the Department of State on the same date.]</p>
                <p>The Secretary of War directs that the Governor of the Panama Canal Zone be
                    informed in substance as follows:</p>
                <p>General Luis Mena, recent revolutionary leader in Nicaragua, has surrendered to
                    Rear Admiral Southerland and will arrive at <pb facs="1170" n="1054"
                        xml:id="pg1054"/> Panama from Corinto on the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cleveland</hi> on October 1st, accompanied by his son
                    and body servant. Rear-Admiral Southerland recommends: “Please request Governor
                    of Canal Zone to permit his landing. I suggest that he be kept under
                    surveillance, and that neither he nor his son be allowed to go to any Central
                    American stale hereafter. He is sick and unable to walk, may have to be sent to
                    Ancon hospital.”</p>
                <p>The Secretary of State considers it important that General Mena and his son
                    understand that the Government of the United States expects them to remain upon
                    Canal Zone territory for the present.</p>
                <p>The Secretary of War directs that you take appropriate action to carry out the
                    wishes of the Secretary of State in connection with the Legation of Panama,
                    which has been directed to cooperate with you.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. W. Wotherspoon.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
